content.text:
The fix that actually solved the instability was in `SimulatorEngine.cs`'s Tier 1/2 branch (around line 182-197):

```csharp
if (pt3d.Tier == TriangulationTier.Monocular && pt3d.Z == 0)
{
    if (!_tracker.HasFix)
    {
        // coast instead of enriching with a meaningless Z
        _coastFrames++;
        if (_coastFrames > _maxCoastFrames) continue;
        pt3d = new TriangulatedPoint { X = 0, Y = 0, Z = 0, Tier = TriangulationTier.KalmanOnly, ... };
    }
    else
    {
        var (kx, ky, kz, _, _, _) = _tracker.LastState;
        pt3d = _triangulator.TriangulateMonocular(det0.Center, kz);
    }
}
```

Before this: whenever `TriangulateStereo` internally rejected a measurement (common in the first few frames of a shot), the code tried to "enrich" it using the tracker's own last known Z — but before any real fix exists, that's just 0. So it was feeding fabricated `(0,0,0)` points straight into the velocity seed, every time, for every shot's first several frames. The seed's least-squares fit was being computed from garbage input, not real triangulated positions — no amount of fixing the *fitting math* could fix that.

The fix: check `_tracker.HasFix` first. If no real fix exists yet, don't fake one — just coast (skip this cycle's data) until a genuine stereo measurement actually lands. Once it does, the seed buffer only ever contains real positions.

That's it — one `if/else` gating an existing code path behind a flag that already existed (`HasFix`, added earlier for the same reason in a different branch). Everything else in that last round (sub-pixel centroid, lower mock noise) was real but secondary — this was the one actually corrupting the data.

