// Triangulator.TriangulateMonocular computes X/Y from a single camera's pixel
// position PLUS the tracker's own last-known Z — not an independent Z
// measurement. That Z then gets fed straight back into the Kalman filter via
// Update(), so during a sustained run of monocular-only cycles (common once
// real stereo agreement gets shaky further from the camera), nothing ever
// independently confirms Z — the filter just echoes back whatever it already
// believed. Any small error in the Z-velocity estimate never gets corrected
// and compounds unbounded the longer the streak runs. Found live: a faster
// (25 m/s) mock shot spent longer in this state per real-time interval and
// the final result diverged to a nonsensical (10.6, 6.6, 55.4) position —
// an 18 m/s shot showed the same declining-confidence pattern, just not
// severely enough to visibly diverge within its shorter tracked window.
// Capping how many CONSECUTIVE non-independent fixes get fed to Update()
// before falling back to Predict()-only coasting bounds how far this can
// drift, matching (but distinct from) the existing _coastFrames idea for
// genuine "no detection at all" cycles.
private const int MaxConsecutiveMonocular = 15;
private int _consecutiveMonocularTicks = 0;

